text:
I've already committed to Austria on the Lepanto setup, and the messaging with France and Turkey has been smooth. I should send a final confirmation to Austria to lock in coordination, and perhaps a brief closing note to Turkey to keep them off-guard about my actual intentions (F NAP to ION is aggressive toward them). France and Germany don't need messages this round.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "All confirmed on my end — F NAP to ION, A ROM to APU, and A VEN holds to cover Trieste if needed. Let's keep Trieste completely quiet and focus our energy on Turkey together. Looking forward to coordinating the Fall push.",
  "TURKEY": "Just wanted to close out our pre-Spring talks on a friendly note — I'm keeping things calm on my end and moving carefully. I hope we can continue building trust as the year progresses."
}

Looking at the situation: Austria wants Greece via ALB-GRE supported by SER, and asked me to keep Trieste quiet. Turkey told me they're going for Greece too (and Romania). I told Austria I wanted ION-GRE, but now I'm reconsidering — taking Tunis is a guaranteed neutral center, while Greece risks a bounce with Turkey. I should let Austria have Greece (they have the support lined up), take Tunis safely, and keep Austria friendly for the Lepanto. I should also reassure Turkey that I'm not going for Greece to avoid friction, and confirm with Austria that I'll support their Greece move rather than compete.

MESSAGES:
{
  "AUSTRIA": "I've reconsidered my Fall plans — I'll be convoying A APU to Tunis and keeping F ION in support rather than competing for Greece. Please go ahead with your ALB-GRE move; I won't be in your way. Let's keep building toward the Lepanto together in 1902.",
  "TURKEY": "Just a heads up — I'm heading to Tunis this fall, not Greece, so there's no risk of us bouncing in the Aegean. I hope we can continue our calm relationship; I'd rather we find common ground than get into an early conflict."
}
